======================================================================
PART C: WHY EXACTLY 7 AND 21 ARE FORBIDDEN
======================================================================

  Checking achievability of (alpha_1, alpha_2) pairs:
  H=23 achievable at n<=6
  H=25 achievable at n<=6
  H=23 achievable at n=7(sampled)
  H=25 achievable at n=7(sampled)

  STRUCTURAL ANALYSIS:
  H = 1 + 2*S where S = alpha_1 + 2*alpha_2 + 4*alpha_3 + ...
  For H to be forbidden, ALL decompositions of S must be blocked.

  H=7:  S=3. Decomps: (3,0) [blocked: alpha_1=3 + no disjoint => c5 forces more]
               (1,1) [impossible: alpha_2=1 needs alpha_1>=2]

  H=21: S=10. Decomps: (10,0),(8,1),(6,2),(4,3),(2,4),(0,5)
               ALL blocked by independent arguments (THM-079)

  H=23: S=11. Decomps: (11,0),(9,1),(7,2),(5,3),(3,4),(1,5)
               (11,0) IS achievable at n=7 (many tournaments)

  PATTERN: S=3 and S=10 are the ONLY values where ALL decompositions
  are simultaneously blocked. For S >= 11, the number of decompositions
  grows, and at least one is always achievable for large enough n.

  CONJECTURE: 7 and 21 are the only forbidden H values because
  they are the only values where every (alpha_1, alpha_2, ...) decomposition
  is independently blocked by a structural constraint.

  Running Part A (Walsh degree at n=7) — this takes ~5-10 min...
